WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:07.479
With his dome helmet, purple cloak, and fishnet-covered
green bodysuit, Mysterio's an instantly recognizable

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:07.980
supervillain.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.380
But just because you can recognize him on
sight doesn't mean you know everything about

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.800
the arachnid-hating trickster who's been one
of Marvel's most tenacious villains.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:23.119
Of everyone who's ever worn Mysterio's fishbowl
helmet, Quentin Beck — created by Steve

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.000
Ditko and Stan Lee — is by far the most
well-known.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:31.480
“Here to save the world from the evils of
technology corrupting the human spirit.”

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.860
“...Are we being punked?”

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.500
Originally a stuntman and makeup artist who
turned to crime after getting frustrated with

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.860
the movie industry, Beck first appeared in
Amazing Spider-Man #13.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.780
His first step in taking down Spider-Man was
to impersonate the masked webhead by using

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.580
his technological prowess to mimic Spidey's
powers and frame him for various crimes.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.540
With Spider-Man's popularity at an all-time
low, Mysterio could swoop in as a hero when

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.500
he took down Spider-Man along with keeping
the money he'd stolen earlier.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:08.640
As you'd expect, Spider-Man took down Mysterio
and cleared his name, but Mysterio would continue

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.210
to pop up over the years, including a role
as one of the original members of the Sinister

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:13.700
Six.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.280
However, one of his most interesting roles
came in 1980, when Beck was revealed as having

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:24.610
actually appeared way back in the second issue
of Amazing Spider-Man, disguised as one of

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.120
a crew of aliens in a truly bizarre early
story.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.080
This helped to establish what is arguably
Mysterio's greatest strength as a character:

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.100
he can make any story work with his mastery
of illusions… no matter how weird it might

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:38.600
get.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:44.620
Mysterio's focus is usually less about killing
Spider-Man than trying to drive him insane,

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.840
a quirk that shows up very early in the character's
identity.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:56.229
In 1965's Amazing Spider-Man #24, Mysterio's
dedication to the long con is truly demonstrated

00:01:56.229 --> 00:02:02.100
when Dr. Ludwig Rinehart arrives at the Daily
Bugle, making a compelling case that Spider-Man

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.100
is a hair's breadth away from complete insanity.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.179
Spidey shrugs it off at first, but when he
begins to see his arch-villains crawling out

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.860
of the walls to attack him before disappearing
in smoke, he's a bit more convinced.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.530
He pays Dr. Rinehart a visit to see if he
can get some help, but the hallucinations

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:24.730
get so bad that Spider-Man gets one step away
from revealing his secret identity.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:30.830
Fortunately, Daily Bugle boss J. Jonah Jameson
bursts in to accuse the not-so-good doctor

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:32.340
of being a fraud.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.349
With his mind clear, Spidey quickly defeats
Dr. Rinehart and discovers, as you might expect,

00:02:37.349 --> 00:02:39.840
that it's actually Quentin Beck in disguise.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:46.319
While Beck's plan wasn't successful, it established
his modus operandi early on — Mysterio could

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:51.930
never beat Spider-Man in a fair fight, so
he uses deception to drive the wall-crawler

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:52.930
to defeat himself.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.110
Mysterio, master of existentialism

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:59.370
Another notable attempt at getting Spidey
to crack came in 1992's Web of Spider-Man

00:02:59.370 --> 00:02:59.860
#90.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:05.260
In the issue, Spider-Man finds himself taking
on the world-devouring Galactus, and wakes

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:10.450
up afterwards to discover that he's a card-carrying,
A-list celebrity who's been enjoying the good

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:15.140
life ever since his brief stint in the wrestling
ring against Crusher Hogan — an event that

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.950
happened way back in his very first appearance.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:22.590
Something's amiss on the set of his newest
feature film, when some of his toughest villains

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:23.080
attack.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.060
After dispatching Hobgoblin and Venom, Spidey
figures out what's really going on.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:33.700
Mysterio hired Max Shiffman to help him construct
an entire alternate life for Spider-Man to

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:34.580
get trapped in.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.840
While the plot is classic Mysterio, the issue
is notable for an additional wrinkle that

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.120
it provides to Mysterio's character.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:46.340
While fighting Spider-Man, Mysterio, disguised
as Venom, reveals that when his helmet was

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:51.629
cracked during their first fight, it exposed
Quentin to the hallucinogenic mists that lets

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:53.370
him control illusions.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:58.680
With his exposure to the mists, his own grasp
on reality was shattered, leaving him unable

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.340
to tell illusion from reality.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:05.100
It's an interesting idea, but it’s only
occasionally been revisited since.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.420
The Marvel Universe has always been connected.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.580
The very first issue of Amazing Spider-Man
has him dropping by to hassle the Fantastic

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:19.590
Four, and Spidey and Daredevil in particular
have an especially long bad guy exchange program.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:25.250
The Kingpin, Daredevil's greatest and most
well-known arch-enemy, was originally a Spider-Man

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:30.760
villain, and The Owl, Electro, and, of course,
Mysterio have all jumped back and forth between

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:31.260
the heroes.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.240
In "Guardian Devil," a 1998 arc of Daredevil
written by filmmaker Kevin Smith and drawn

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:42.980
by future Marvel Editor-in-Chief Joe Quesada,
Daredevil wrestles with the responsibility

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:47.449
of taking care of a child that seems to be
either the Messiah or the Anti-Christ.

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:53.790
However, after a quick consultation with Dr.
Strange and Mephisto — the actual, literal

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:58.180
Devil in the Marvel Universe — he realizes
that he's been drugged and that the baby is

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.040
just a baby.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:04.750
Mysterio, newly diagnosed with lung cancer
and an inoperable brain tumor from his years

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:09.950
of getting high off of his own hallucinogenic
supply, had enacted a grand revenge scheme

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:12.080
against his second-most hated foe.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.840
Once the trick was revealed, Mysterio was
hopeful that Daredevil would kill him.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.250
Once that failed, Mysterio took care of the
matter himself, in a death that spelled the

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.960
end for Quentin Beck's run as Mysterio…
for a while.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:29.100
“I always did like a good cliffhanger.”

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.120
While there are other people who have worn
the Mysterio mantle in the main Marvel Universe,

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:40.650
also known as Earth-616, there's another version
worth mentioning: the Mysterio of the Ultimate

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:41.280
Universe.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:46.820
While other characters would get a complete
top-to-bottom reimagining for Marvel’s rebooted

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:52.380
Ultimate comics, the Ultimate version of Mysterio
wasn't all that different from the 616 version.

00:05:53.009 --> 00:05:58.120
He first showed up in Ultimate Spider-Man
Annual #3, and his biggest difference from

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.420
his 616 counterpart was that he had a worse
costume.

00:06:01.889 --> 00:06:07.080
While some would find fault with a fishnet-covered
green bodysuit and fishbowl helmet, you can't

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:10.070
argue that it isn't immediately recognizable.

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:15.449
The Ultimate version of Mysterio had none
of his predecessor's flair, using only a boring

00:06:15.449 --> 00:06:18.180
ghost-like effect over his face as a disguise.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:22.820
As it turns out, though, there was a reason
that the Ultimate Mysterio wasn't all that

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:24.800
different from the 616 version.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:30.780
In a truly weird plot twist, it was eventually
revealed that they weren't actually two characters

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:31.979
at all.

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:38.240
Instead, the Ultimate Mysterio was a ridiculously
high-tech android piloted across universes

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:45.139
by the 616 Mysterio in order to… accomplish
the same kinds of crimes that he got up to

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:46.060
in his home universe.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:47.400
No, really.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.300
It happened in Spider-Men, a series where
the core universe’s Peter Parker traveled

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:58.040
across dimensions to mentor the Ultimate Universe’s
new Spider-Man, Miles Morales.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:03.520
If that's all sounding just a bit familiar,
it's because the plot was loosely adapted

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:09.419
for 2018's Into the Spider-Verse motion picture,
along with a couple of other cross-dimensional

00:07:09.419 --> 00:07:10.840
Spider-Man stories.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:15.370
Spider-Men ended with Mysterio in custody
in the Ultimate Universe, leaving the role

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:18.720
of Mysterio to be filled by other, lesser
criminal geniuses.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.870
Quentin Beck is the original, but he’s not
the only Mysterio.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:30.229
In Amazing Spider-Man #141, Daniel Berkhart
appears as a stuntman who took over for Beck,

00:07:30.229 --> 00:07:32.660
who had faked his own death at the time.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:37.620
While it's hard for any villainous replacement
to surpass the original, Berkhart was less

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:43.130
notable than most, and never really had a
unique spin on the Mysterio identity.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:48.599
His most well-known stories involved him pretending
to be a resurrected Quentin Beck in order

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.120
to torment Spider-Man.

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:55.240
When your best-known plot involves pretending
to be a more beloved character and generally

00:07:55.259 --> 00:07:59.650
succeeding, you're not really going to last
long in a comic book universe.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:04.550
Even when he got title billing in his own
miniseries, Spider-Man: The Mysterio Manifesto,

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:10.729
he spent most of the story dressed as an entirely
different second-string supervillain: Jack

00:08:10.729 --> 00:08:11.300
O'Lantern.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:12.520
Spooky!

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:19.199
Created by Rachel and Terry Dodson and Kevin
Smith in Spider-Man/Black Cat: The Evil That

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:23.360
Men Do, Francis Klum is arguably the worst
Mysterio.

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.060
“Ah, Spider-Man my nemesis.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:28.160
You won’t catch me this time!”

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:36.600
Unlike others, Klum actually has super-powers:
he's a mutant with the ability to teleport,

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.780
along with some assorted telepathy and telekinesis.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:45.460
In a complicated series of events, Klum ended
up accidentally getting knocked off a bridge

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:51.110
by Spider-Man, which led to Klum losing part
of his leg, and naturally, this led him to

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:55.380
embark on a crusade of vengeance against Spider-Man
dressed as one of his old enemies.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:09:00.240
His crusade of vengeance didn't last long,
though — he was interrupted by both Beck

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:03.740
and Berkhart who were not happy about gimmick
infringement.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:10.280
Even worse for Klum, he was eventually stabbed
in the chest by thousands of spiders wearing

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:13.880
the form of a school nurse that’s comics,
baby!

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:20.059
While Berkhart and Klum popped up to cover
for Beck while he was dead, who fought Spider-Man

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:23.900
while Beck was stuck in the Ultimate Universe
after the events of Spider-Men?

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.080
A brand new character called Mysterion.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:30.200
Created by David Lopez and Christopher Yost

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:35.580
in Avenging Spider-Man #22 - which was the 23rd issue because... you know what? just forget it.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.360
Comics... am I right? Anyway... Mysterion was…

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:43.200
well, basically the same as previous Mysterios,
except not quite as competent.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.980
In the issue, he picks up the costume and
gear from Hobgoblin before running afoul of

00:09:47.999 --> 00:09:53.509
both the Punisher, who kills supervillains,
and the Superior Spider-Man, a Spider-Man

00:09:53.509 --> 00:09:57.620
with the soul of Doctor Octopus who also kills
supervillains.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.940
Somewhat surprisingly, he actually managed
to survive the encounter and popped up a couple

00:10:02.949 --> 00:10:07.739
more times in the Marvel Universe — most
notably when he took on the unbeatable Squirrel

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:11.140
Girl and all of the Avengers with robot dinosaurs.

00:10:11.269 --> 00:10:16.420
Unfortunately, without an actual name or defining
features, we're unlikely to see much more

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.900
of Mysterion going forward.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:23.779
Considering how closely his power set aligns
with movie magic, you would have expected

00:10:23.779 --> 00:10:30.459
Mysterio to make an appearance in a cinematic
Spider-Man adaptation long before 2019's Spider-Man:

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:31.160
Far From Home.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.280
As it turns out, he quite nearly did.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:38.730
Jeffrey Henderson, a storyboard artist who
worked with Sam Raimi on the original cinematic

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:43.480
Spider-Man trilogy, revealed that a planned
Spider-Man 4 would have included Mysterio,

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:45.500
along with a handful of other Spider-Foes.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:52.720
Apparently the idea was to run through lower-tier
villains like the Shocker, the Prowler, Rhino,

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:57.089
Stilt-Man, and, of course, Mysterio, since
they figured they would never be chosen as

00:10:57.089 --> 00:10:59.300
the main antagonists of other films.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:05.060
As for who would portray the master of illusions
in the quick cameo, Henderson suggested that

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:11.420
Bruce Campbell, a longtime Sam Raimi collaborator
who had made brief cameos in all of the previous

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:15.060
Spider-Man movies, could step beneath the
bowl to play Quentin Beck.

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:19.920
It never happened, but it’s not hard not
to be intrigued by what the scenery-chewing

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:23.600
Campbell could've done with one of Mysterio's
many monologues.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:24.580
“Ah, Romance!

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:28.300
I am French!”
“...uh.”

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:33.399
It took him a long time to get his big-screen
due, but Mysterio finally made the jump to

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:38.880
theaters courtesy of Jake Gyllenhaal's portrayal
in 2019's Spider-Man: Far from Home.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:42.560
In some ways, his long journey to the big
screen is a good thing.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:47.179
With the huge advances in CGI and special
effects technology made in the last couple

00:11:47.179 --> 00:11:52.100
of decades, there's arguably no better time
for a supervillain to harness the lifelike

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:54.760
illusions and movie magic trickery of Mysterio.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:12:01.000
Plus, given Gyllenhaal's deeply upsetting
portrayal of an amoral psychopath in 2014's

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:06.259
Nightcrawler, it's almost guaranteed that
the villain will be just as creepy and unsettling

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.280
as his best stories make him out to be.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:13.040
Here's hoping this latest incarnation of this
enduring Marvel character ends up sticking

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.790
around for more than a single outing — and
signals a future in which the MCU's villains

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:19.420
only get weirder and wilder.

